  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nWe optimize a sequence for minimal cross-correlation energy by representing it as eigenmodes of a Toeplitz covariance matrix. These modes inherit the periodicity inherent in the step function problem structure, offering better convergence properties than gradient methods alone. The optimization uses a form of alternating direction implicit (ADI) scheme for covariance matrix inversion, preserving non-negativity constraints during iteration. This method leverages the fact that cross-correlation extrema are optimally managed in domain-specific eigenforms.\n\nMathematical Specification:\nFor input sequence a = (a0, a1, ..., an-1):\n  Let A be the n\u00d7n Toeplitz matrix with A[i,j] = a_{|i-j|} \n  Then, we seek sequence a such that a = A\u03c8 / ||A\u03c8||, where \u03c8 is an n-dimensional complex-valued vector obtained via an unconstrained minimization of the functional f(\u03c8) = (1/(n*sum_magnitude^2)) * max_i (real(\u03c8_bar * (A\u03c8)_i)) \nThe method alternates between two symmetric positive definite matrices derived from A's structure.\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Initialize \u03c8 from either provided parent sequence (if available) or a random unit complex vector (setting real part = cos, imag part = sin).\n2. Normalize \u03c8 using a fixed threshold to ensure real part dominates.\n3. Implement ADI solver (with two consecutive imaginary directions) for matrix inversion on a Toeplitz structure that incorporates autocorrelation.\n4. Underlying optimization:\n    maximize min(1-convolution) subject to total sum normalization\n    via complex vector reparameterization.\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n  - Dimensional Expansion: Start at n=4096 and try n=512, 1024, 2048, 4096.\n  - Phase: Use real part initialization before ADI, then full complex to get symmetry.\n\nComputational Budget and Robustness:\n  - Start with n=512, then progress to 1024, etc.\n  - Implement early termination for matrix inversions that clearly converge.\n  - Use temporal budget mapping to network idle time for the core optimization.\n\nValidation and Return Contract:\n  - Central execution point returns a normalized real sequence.\n  - Use exact function from evaluation but fallback to uniform temperature profiles.\n  - Profile evaluation: Multiply by 1/(2\u03c0) adjustment factor.\n  - Ensure non-negative and finite sequence using extreme value function if needed.",
